d=Document(f)
anchor=next(p for p in d.paragraphs if p.text.strip().casefold() == '5.2. future work')
text=("Interface and API evolution should be planned as a coordinated contract change. The existing browser obtains history through a REST request and then subscribes to the room's STOMP topic; if the server changes a message field or timestamp format, both paths must continue to produce records that the same rendering component understands. A versioned response contract, explicit date-time format and shared examples would make this expectation reviewable. The client should also distinguish a stale history snapshot from a live event already received during loading, using a stable message identifier to reconcile the two sources. Its current composite duplicate key uses sender, time and a content prefix; this reduces some overlap but cannot prove identity when two messages share those values. Introducing a server-generated ID would allow reliable reconciliation, editing, deletion and moderation audit trails if those features were later designed.\n\nRoom lifecycle requires similar care. The current create operation establishes a room document, while entering a room checks its existence and message submission resolves it again. This is a simple lifecycle that avoids implicit creation during posting, but it does not define ownership, expiry, invitation or deletion. Future room controls should specify who can create, join, rename or close a room and what happens to retained messages when a room is removed. These decisions affect data retention and user expectations, so they should precede interface controls. Participant presence currently represents active WebSocket sessions rather than durable membership; a participant list should therefore not be presented as an access-control list. The distinction between being allowed to enter and being connected at this moment is fundamental to an accurate multi-user experience.\n\nTransient event behavior also benefits from explicit policy. Typing notifications are intentionally short-lived and do not belong in message history. The client sends activity as the user types and stops after inactivity; receiving clients clear indicators after a timeout. A future implementation could suppress redundant frames, handle tab suspension and disconnection explicitly, and ensure that a user's own typing event is not shown back to that same user. Such optimizations should preserve the principle that typing is advisory: it must never block message delivery or be treated as evidence that a message was sent. Participant events, in contrast, should be reconciled after reconnect so that the UI can recover a fresh snapshot rather than relying on missed incremental changes. These details are suitable future refinements, not additional behavior claimed for the current code.")
